@startuml hld-02a_module_layers
title HLD-02a — Inside one module, HIGH VIEW (UML **package diagram**)\nDependencies are UML package dependencies; the red one carries a UML constraint {forbidden} and is what the guard fails on.\nWhat each package contains, and what it may reach outside the module, is HLD-02b.
skinparam packageStyle rectangle
skinparam wrapWidth 210

package "modules::<module_id>" <<Bounded Context>> {
  package contracts <<Public>> #LightGreen {
  }
  package ui <<Presentation>> #White {
  }
  package adapters <<Infrastructure>> #White {
  }
  package application <<Use Cases>> #White {
  }
  package domain <<Enterprise Rules>> #White {
  }
  class "module.py" as ModulePy <<BoundedContextModule>> {
    +register(context)
    +boot(context)
    +contribute(registry)
    +subscribe(bridge)
  }
}

ui ..> application : <<access>>
adapters ..> application : <<access>>
application ..> domain : <<access>>
application ..> contracts : <<import>>
ModulePy ..> ui : <<access>>
ModulePy ..> adapters : <<access>>
ModulePy ..> application : <<access>>
ModulePy ..> domain : <<access>>
ui .[#Red].> adapters : <<access>>\n**{forbidden}**

note right of ui
  **{forbidden}: ui never imports adapters.** A presenter asks the
  application layer, which resolves a port; it never reaches for the
  exchange client itself. Two violations exist today —
  `backtest_presenter.py:43` and `settings_presenter.py:21` — and are
  fixed when those modules migrate.
end note

note bottom of ModulePy
  `module.py` is the only classifier in the module that depends on all
  four layers; it is the module's single point of assembly.
end note

note bottom of contracts
  Any dependency not drawn here is forbidden. The guard (architecture-rule §3,
  generalised in Phase 0 to `modules/*/{domain,application,adapters,ui}`)
  fails the build on one.
end note
@enduml
